<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="assimilatory Fe(III) reduction (plants)" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260303222105">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-5934" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Arabidopsis thaliana col&lt;/i&gt;: Enzyme(s) that catalyzes one or more of the key reactions in this pathway were predicted in Arabidopsis thaliana col. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; Iron deficiency effects all aspects of plant development, including photosynthesis, respiration, and growth processes. Though abundant in soil, iron is frequently found in the form of insoluble oxides and oxyhydroxides, and is not available for direct uptake. Plants have evolved several tightly regulated strategies to absorb iron. Chelation of |FRAME: FE+3| by |FRAME: Phytosiderophores phytosiderophores&quot;| secreted from the plant roots is one such strategy |CITS: [17449639]|. Under low Fe conditions the enzyme |FRAME: AT5G49740-MONOMER| is induced and reduces the chelated |FRAME: FE+3| to |FRAME: FE+2|, which is taken into the plant by Fe-regulated transporters in the roots |CITS: [GUERINOT07]|. This process is widespread in non graminaceous plants.</Description>
  <Authors>
    <Author name="O-27" />
    <Author name="pujar" />
  </Authors>
  <Comment source="Automated Conversion">This GPML file was automatically generated from PlantCyc flatfiles.</Comment>
